        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Mistake",
            "Damage"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "return True in __exit__",
              "eats every exception"
            ],
            [
              "bare yield, no finally",
              "teardown skipped on raise"
            ],
            [
              "reuse one @contextmanager",
              "RuntimeError on 2nd entry"
            ],
            [
              "acquire in __init__",
              "leaks if never entered"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Partial Setup Leaks",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "__enter__ that raises after acquiring #1 leaks it",
            "__exit__ never runs, so nothing releases #1",
            "Fix: hold an internal ExitStack while setting up"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "def __enter__(self):",
            "    with ExitStack() as s:",
            "        self.f = s.enter_context(open(p))",
            "        self.c = s.enter_context(connect())",
            "        self._stack = s.pop_all()   # safe"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Scope Discipline"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`suppress(Exception)` over a big block hides real failures",
            "It also skips the rest of the block silently",
            "`with` never swallows errors — it guarantees cleanup only",
            "Holding a lock for a whole function hurts concurrency",
            "Deep nesting is right-drift with no added safety"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Choosing an Approach",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Tool",
            "Best for",
            "Catch"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "try/finally",
              "one-off cleanup",
              "repeated everywhere"
            ],
            [
              "class",
              "reuse, state",
              "more code"
            ],
            [
              "@contextmanager",
              "linear pair",
              "single-use"
            ],
            [
              "ExitStack",
              "dynamic count",
              "explicit calls"
            ],
            [
              "suppress",
              "discarding only",
              "very narrow"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Decision Rule"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Fixed and few: `with a, b`",
            "Runtime-variable count: ExitStack",
            "Simple pair of steps: @contextmanager",
            "Needs state or reuse: write a class",
            "All-or-nothing construction: pop_all()"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "The Contract",
          "text": "__enter__ raises: no __exit__. Otherwise __exit__ always runs. A truthy __exit__ return suppresses. Cleanup order is LIFO in every mechanism."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Cost and Symmetry",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`with` adds about two method calls per block",
            "Irrelevant next to I/O; measure only in hot loops",
            "Async mirrors sync one for one",
            "__aenter__/__aexit__, @asynccontextmanager",
            "AsyncExitStack, aclosing — same shapes"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Async Rules, Stdlib Managers",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Design Guidance"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Make each manager do exactly one thing",
            "Keep __exit__ non-throwing and idempotent",
            "Never suppress by default"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Not Just For Files",
          "text": "`with` is Python's general mechanism for guaranteeing a paired action completes. Any set/restore, begin/commit or enter/leave belongs inside one."
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "cm = mgr()",
            "with cm: pass",
            "with cm: pass   # RuntimeError:",
            "                # generator didn't yield"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Partial Setup Leaks",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "__enter__ grabs #1, then raises grabbing #2",
            "__exit__ never runs, so #1 leaks",
            "Fix: hold an internal ExitStack inside __enter__",
            "pop_all() only once every acquire succeeded"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Two Wrong Beliefs"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`with suppress(Exception):` over a big block hides failures",
            "And silently skips the rest of the block",
            "`with` does NOT swallow exceptions — it guarantees cleanup",
            "Holding a lock for a whole function hurts concurrency"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Last Three Traps",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "sqlite3 conn as manager commits, never closes",
            "Wrap it: `with closing(connect(db)) as c:` too",
            "`with` on async manager, `async with` on sync — both TypeError",
            "Deep nesting = right-drift with zero added safety"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "with closing(sqlite3.connect(db)) as c:",
            "    with c:              # commits/rolls back",
            "        c.execute(sql)   # outer also closes"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Pick Your Mechanism",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Tool",
            "Best for",
            "Weakness"
          ],
          "rows": [
